# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/clubs_jobs.grim
# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after "="; keep {tokens}.
# "# en:" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;
# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.


@event duelling_ward_line
# en: The Ward Line
title [56f9d8] = 护盾阵线
# en: {Ward drill. Members hold shields in a line while the seniors lob stinging hexes down the hall.|You know by now which senior aims low, and which one apologises.|You could hold a shield in your sleep, and one night you did.} {A dummy at the end of the line has a shield of its own, out of pity.|Someone has chalked a tally of scorched floorboards. It is long.|A first-year holds the shield upside down and does surprisingly well.}
text [86fffa] = {护盾演练。社员排成一列举着护盾，高年级生从大厅那头往这边扔刺痛咒。|你早就知道哪位学长专往低处打，哪位打完还会道歉。|你闭着眼都能举盾，有一晚还真在梦里举了一整夜。} {队尾的假人也被分到一面盾，纯属同情。|有人用粉笔记下了烧焦的地板数，那一串很长。|一个一年级生把盾举反了，居然撑得出奇地好。}
# en: A row of students holding shimmering shields across a scorched hall while seniors lob sparks at them.
scene [271b31] = 一排学生在焦黑的大厅里举着微光闪烁的护盾，高年级生朝他们抛掷火花。
# en: Take the front of the line
opt.left.label [d28df6] = 站到队伍最前面
# en: {Your shield takes everything and hums about it afterwards.|Hexes splash off your ward like rain off a good hat.|You hold until the bell. {around(lyra): Lyra|The senior on the whistle} writes something down, which is either praise or a list of your faults.}
opt.left.say.1 [c1da68] = {你的护盾照单全收，事后还嗡嗡响了好一阵。|咒语从你的护界上溅开，像雨水打在一顶好帽子上。|你一直撑到铃响。{around(lyra): 莱拉|吹哨的高年级生}记了点什么，要么是夸奖，要么是你的毛病清单。}
# en: {A hex finds the gap by your elbow. Your arm fizzes until supper.|Your ward folds like wet paper. The senior apologises, mostly to the floor.|You hold the front half asleep, as usual, and a hex wakes you. Your arm fizzes until supper, and the first-year beside you holds.}
opt.left.say.2 [4d8fe9] = {一道咒语钻进你手肘边的空隙，整条胳膊麻到晚饭。|你的护盾像湿纸一样折了。那位学长道了歉，主要是对着地板。|你照常半睡半醒地守在前排，一道恶咒把你惊醒了。你的胳膊一直麻到晚饭，旁边那个一年级生却守住了。}
# en: Hold the back row tonight
opt.right.label [a28ef7] = 今晚待在后排
# en: {From the back you watch how the good shields bend instead of break. Nobody watches you.|You spend the drill studying the front row's mistakes, which feels useful and looks like hiding.|Safe at the back, you learn a great deal about the ceiling.}
opt.right.say.1 [b86875] = {在后排，你看清了好护盾是弯而不折的。没人看你。|你整场都在研究前排的失误，感觉很有用，看起来很像在躲。|在安全的后排，你对天花板有了深入了解。}
# en: Hold the line with a real ward
opt.up.label [c704b4] = 用真正的护界守住阵线
# en: {You raise a proper ward, the one from your first week, and the seniors' hexes slide off it like rain off glass. {around(lyra): Lyra stops the drill to look.|The seniors stop the drill to look.}|Your ward holds the whole line's flank. Your arm aches from the mana, not the hexes.|You raise the real ward without thinking, the way you hold a shield in your sleep. The seniors give up hexing your end of the line.}
opt.up.say.1 [b916f3] = {你立起一道真正的护界，就是入学第一周学的那种，高年级生的恶咒从上面滑开，像雨水滑过玻璃。{around(lyra): 莱拉停下训练来看。|高年级生们停下训练来看。}|你的护界守住了整条阵线的侧翼。你的胳膊酸痛，是因为法力，不是因为恶咒。|你不假思索就立起了真正的护界，就像你睡着都能举盾一样。高年级生们放弃了朝你这头放恶咒。}

@event duelling_dummy_mending
# en: Mending the Dummies
title [d3be80] = 缝补假人
# en: {After practice someone has to mend the dummies, and tonight the rota says it's you.|Mending night again, and the rota has your name on it.|You know every dummy here by its scars. They look at you with something like trust.} {The tall dummy is missing an arm. Someone always takes the arm.|One dummy is now mostly scorch mark.|Someone has painted a new name on a dummy's chest, and a grudge beneath it.}
text [556795] = {练习结束后总得有人缝补假人，值日表上今晚写的是你。|又是缝补之夜，值日表上又有你的名字。|这里每个假人身上的伤疤你都认得。它们看你的眼神有点像信任了。} {那个高个子假人少了一条胳膊。总有人把胳膊拿走。|有个假人现在基本只剩焦痕。|有人在一个假人胸口画了新名字，下面还写着一笔旧怨。}
# en: A student alone in a duelling hall at night, stitching straw back into a battered practice dummy.
scene [29efd2] = 夜里，一个学生独自坐在决斗厅里，把稻草塞回一个破旧的练习假人。
# en: Stay late and stitch them up
opt.left.label [84da41] = 留下来把它们缝好
# en: {By midnight every dummy is whole and you have straw in places straw should not be.|You sew one dummy's arm on slightly backwards. It looks more dangerous for it.|The hall is quiet, the needle is steady, and for an hour nobody is shouting about footwork.}
opt.left.say.1 [2293d0] = {到午夜，所有假人都完好如初，你身上也多了几处不该有稻草的地方。|你把一个假人的胳膊缝反了。它看起来反倒更危险了。|大厅很安静，针脚很稳，整整一个钟头没人嚷嚷步法。}
# en: Leave them for the morning
opt.right.label [3c93be] = 留到明早再说
# en: {In the morning the dummies are still in pieces, and so is the seniors' patience.|You slip out early. Someone finds the dummies and puts your name on next week's rota, underlined twice.|You leave them. In the morning the dummy with the new name has been mended by someone else, with your name painted under the grudge.}
opt.right.say.1 [552491] = {第二天早上，假人们还是一地零件，高年级生们的耐心也是。|你提前溜了。有人发现了这些假人，把你的名字写进下周值日表，还画了两道线。|你没管它们。第二天早上，那个写着新名字的假人被别人补好了，怨气下面还被画上了你的名字。}

@event duelling_refereeing_lyra
# en: Refereeing Lyra
title [9a9369] = 给莱拉当裁判
# en: {Officers referee, so tonight you hold the whistle while Lyra duels a second-year.|You know the circle's chalk by heart now, and Lyra's footwork better.|{rel(lyra) >= 12: Lyra no longer asks who is refereeing. She looks for you.|The whistle has a dent in it from some earlier, unrecorded argument.}} {Her last spell landed a breath after the bell.|She stepped out of the circle by a boot's width, and knows it.|Her last hex went in after her opponent had called the yield.} {final_year: One of the last bouts you will call for her.|}
text [e5a2df] = {干部要当裁判，于是今晚你拿着哨子，看莱拉和一个二年级生对决。|决斗圈的粉笔线你已烂熟于心，莱拉的步法你更熟。|{rel(lyra) >= 12: 莱拉已经不问谁当裁判了。她直接找你。|哨子上有个凹痕，来自某次没记录在案的争执。}} {她最后一道咒语比铃声晚了一口气。|她踏出圈外一只靴子宽，她自己也知道。|她最后一道恶咒是在对手喊了认输之后才打出去的。} {final_year: 这是你最后几次为她判比赛了。|}
# en: A student with a whistle watching a noble duellist in a chalk circle, her opponent's shield just failing.
scene [a5fea0] = 一个拿着哨子的学生注视着圈中的贵族决斗者，她对手的护盾正要崩溃。
# en: Call it against her
opt.left.label [dff616] = 判她犯规
# en: {"Noted," Lyra says, in a voice that could chill wine. Next day her feet are perfect.|Lyra stares at you, then shakes her opponent's hand. Later a cup of tea appears on your bench.|The hall goes silent. Lyra bows to the second-year, very correctly, and to you, slightly less.}
opt.left.say.1 [f1b129] = {“记下了。”莱拉说，那声音能把酒冰镇。第二天，她的步法无懈可击。|莱拉盯着你看了好一会儿，然后和对手握了手。后来你的长凳上出现了一杯茶。|大厅一片寂静。莱拉向那个二年级生行礼，极其标准；向你行礼，稍逊一些。}
# en: She would never have forgiven you for letting it stand, and you both know why: you're the one person here who makes her better.
opt.left.say.2 [940131] = 你要是让它算数，她永远不会原谅你，你们俩都知道为什么：你是这里唯一能让她变得更好的人。
# en: Let it stand
opt.right.label [d81aa7] = 维持原判
# en: {Lyra wins. The second-year looks at you, and you find something interesting about your whistle.|Nobody objects. Lyra does not thank you; if anything, she looks a little disappointed in you.|The bout goes in the book as hers. It sits there all week like a stone in your shoe.}
opt.right.say.1 [f9ce12] = {莱拉赢了。那个二年级生看向你，你忽然觉得自己的哨子很有意思。|没人反对。莱拉没有谢你，甚至显得对你有点失望。|这场比赛作为她的胜场记进了册子，整整一周都像鞋里的一颗石子。}

@event duelling_running_practice
# en: Running Practice
title [9e7aed] = 主持练习
# en: A note is pinned to the hall door in Lyra's sharp hand: "Called home. You are running practice. Footwork first. Do not let anyone set the ceiling on fire again." The whole Circle turns to see what you will say.
text [189cd9] = 决斗厅门上钉着一张字条，是莱拉凌厉的笔迹：“家里有事。你来主持练习。先练步法。别再让任何人把天花板点着。”整个决斗社都转过头来，看你要说什么。
# en: A note pinned to a duelling hall door, a crowd of students waiting inside in chalk circles.
scene [6d7864] = 决斗厅门上钉着一张字条，门内一群学生站在粉笔圈里等着。
# en: Run it exactly her way
opt.left.label [b06e03] = 完全照她的方式来
# en: Footwork, then shields, then footwork again. Nobody sets the ceiling on fire. Lyra reads your practice log on her return and says "adequate", then reads it again when she thinks you've gone.
opt.left.say.1 [a1b74e] = 步法，护盾，再练步法。没人把天花板点着。莱拉回来读了你的练习记录，说了句“还行”，又趁她以为你走了的时候读了一遍。
# en: Run it your own way
opt.right.label [68c6ad] = 按你自己的方式来
# en: You turn the footwork drill into a game of tag with stinging hexes. The ceiling only smoulders. A week later Lyra runs the same game and calls it her idea, which is the highest praise she has.
opt.right.say.1 [b32c5f] = 你把步法练习改成了带刺痛咒的捉人游戏。天花板只是冒了点烟。一周后莱拉照搬这个游戏，说是她自己的主意——这是她能给出的最高赞美。

@event lumen_ledger_letters_box
# en: The Letters Box
title [4a9d28] = 读者来信箱
# en: {The letters box is full. Stringers sort it, Nessa says, having no dignity yet to lose.|Sorting day. A regular correspondent has started numbering their letters.|Under the stairs the box has burst its lid. You know the regulars by their handwriting now.} {Porridge complaints, a poem about the lake and a sock.|A furious letter about the crossword, in a professor's hand.|An anonymous tip in lemon juice, noticed only because it smells nice.}
text [547c71] = {读者来信箱满了。内莎说，来信由见习记者来分，因为他们还没有尊严可丢。|分信日。有位常客开始给自己的信编号了。|楼梯下，信箱被撑开了盖。如今你光看笔迹就认得出那些常客。} {抱怨粥的信、一首写湖的诗，还有一只袜子。|一封痛骂填字游戏的信，显然是某位教授的笔迹。|一条用柠檬汁写的匿名线索，你注意到它只因为它闻起来很香。}
# en: A battered wooden box under a library staircase, overflowing with folded letters and one sock.
scene [83935d] = 图书馆楼梯下一只破旧的木箱，塞满了折好的信，还有一只袜子。
# en: Print the angriest letter
opt.left.label [321002] = 刊登最愤怒的那封
# en: {It runs on page four. By breakfast several people are angry about the angry letter, which Nessa calls "engagement".|The letter runs. Its author glares at you over the porridge, as if you had written it.|You print the lemon-juice tip, warmed over a candle. Half the castle holds the page to the fire. Nessa calls it the best-selling issue of term.}
opt.left.say.1 [60d770] = {它登在第四版。到早饭时，已经有好几个人对这封愤怒的信感到愤怒，内莎管这叫“读者互动”。|信登出去了。写信人在早餐桌上瞪着你，好像信是你写的。|你把那条柠檬汁写的线报放在蜡烛上烘出字来，登了出去。半个城堡的人都把那一页凑到火边烤。内莎说这是本学期卖得最好的一期。}
# en: Answer them all by hand
opt.right.label [931b86] = 逐一亲笔回信
# en: {You write back to every one of them, even the ones that weren't really letters. Your hand cramps; somewhere in the castle a lonely first-year gets a reply.|A pile of replies, all polite. Nessa calls it "a waste of a perfectly good grudge", then borrows your phrasing.|You write back to every regular, by number. The numbering correspondent replies with a number of their own, and a thank-you.}
opt.right.say.1 [c7409a] = {你给每一封都回了信，连那些算不上信的也回了。手抽筋了，但城堡某处，一个孤单的一年级生收到了回信。|一摞回信，封封客气。内莎说这是“浪费了一份上好的怨气”，然后借用了你的措辞。|你按编号给每位常客回了信。那位编号的通信者回了一封，也编了号，还附上一句谢谢。}

@event lumen_ledger_courtyard_quotes
# en: A Word from the Courtyard
title [2bef78] = 庭院之声
# en: {Every issue needs a word from the courtyard. One question, many opinions.|Word has got round the courtyard that the Ledger is asking questions.|Half the courtyard now takes the long way round your notebook.} This week: {is the soup getting thinner?|who will win the Spring Tournament?|is the lake louder lately?} {season == "winter": Everyone is hurrying indoors.|{Some second-years pretend not to see you.|A passing professor looks very quotable.|A prefect makes a detour to avoid you, which is a quote of a sort.}}
text [8f39dd] = {每期报纸都要一段“庭院之声”。一个问题，众说纷纭。|庭院里已经传开了，说《流明纪事报》在到处提问。|如今半个庭院见了你的本子都绕道走。} 本周的问题是{汤是不是越来越稀了？|谁会赢得春季锦标赛？|湖最近是不是更吵了？} {season == "winter": 人人都急着往屋里走。|{几个二年级生假装没看见你。|一位路过的教授看上去很值得引用。|一位级长为了躲你特意绕了路，这也算是一句引语。}}
# en: A student with a notebook stopping passers-by in a busy courtyard.
scene [3bd888] = 一个拿着笔记本的学生在热闹的庭院里拦住路人。
# en: Stop strangers and ask
opt.left.label [91c2da] = 拦住陌生人提问
# en: {A gardener, a prefect and a very small first-year give you the best quotes of the term. The first-year's is unprintable, and perfect.|You get a quote so good Nessa moves it to the front page and pretends it was her idea to ask.|You ask the question to the people who take the long way round. They answer, to get rid of you, and the answers are the best yet.}
opt.left.say.1 [b843d5] = {一位园丁、一位级长和一个特别小的一年级生给了你本学期最好的引语。那个一年级生的没法登，但完美。|你拿到一句绝妙的引语，内莎把它挪上头版，还假装是她想到要去问的。|你专问那些绕远路躲你的人。他们为了打发你才回答，答案却是到目前为止最好的。}
# en: {Everyone is busy, cold or suddenly deaf. You come back with "no comment" in five different tones of voice.|A fourth-year answers at length, entirely about himself.|Everyone takes the long way round you. You get one quote, from the prefect, and it is "No."}
opt.left.say.2 [9bebc2] = {人人都忙、都冷、或者突然聋了。你带回了五种语气的“无可奉告”。|一个四年级生滔滔不绝地回答，内容全是他自己。|人人都绕开你走。你只拿到一句引语，来自那位级长，内容是“不”。}
# en: Just quote your friends
opt.right.label [1f9ee3] = 直接引用朋友的话
# en: {Every opinion comes from someone you had lunch with. Nessa reads them and says, "Interesting. Your friends are very consistent."|It's quick. It's also the courtyard as represented by one person in several scarves.|You quote your friends again. Nessa runs it under a headline about the same four people, and it's somehow popular.}
opt.right.say.1 [a242a3] = {每条意见都来自和你一起吃午饭的人。内莎读完说：“有意思。你的朋友们观点很统一。”|又快又省事。只是整个庭院的意见，都由同一个人围着几条不同的围巾代表了。|你又引用了你的朋友。内莎给它配了个关于“还是那四个人”的标题，不知怎么还挺受欢迎。}

@event lumen_ledger_red_ink
# en: Red Ink
title [025680] = 红墨水
# en: {Nessa edits your story with a red quill and no mercy.|Nessa has your draft, and this time the red quill hesitates once before it moves.|Edit night. You have learned to bring two copies, one for Nessa to kill.} "{Kill your darlings. Bury them. Page six.|Lead's buried. Dig it up. Headline it.|Good. Too long. Shorter. Shorter than that.}" She has cut your favourite paragraph.
text [4f9392] = {内莎用一支红羽毛笔毫不留情地改你的稿子。|内莎拿到了你的草稿，这一次，红笔落下前迟疑了一下。|改稿之夜。你学会了带两份稿子，一份留给内莎去杀。} “{杀掉你的心头好。埋了。第六版。|导语埋太深了。挖出来。做标题。|好。太长。再短。比那还短。}”她删掉了你最喜欢的那一段。
# en: A cramped office papered with old front pages, an editor slashing a manuscript with a red quill.
scene [f3d694] = 贴满旧头版的狭小办公室里，一位编辑正用红羽毛笔大刀阔斧地删改稿件。
# en: Fight for the paragraph
opt.left.label [528d97] = 为那一段据理力争
# en: {Nessa reads it again, out loud, and puts it back. "Fine. Stays. Don't get used to it."|You win the paragraph. Nessa makes you cut a different one, as a tax.|You fight for it, and it gets shorter, and shorter than that, and it stays. Nessa calls it "a paragraph now".}
opt.left.say.1 [6f10cf] = {内莎把那段大声念了一遍，又放了回去。“行。留着。别习惯。”|你保住了那一段。内莎让你删掉另一段，当作税。|你为它争，它越来越短，短了又短，但留下了。内莎说“这才算一段”。}
# en: {"No," says Nessa, and that is the whole argument.|She listens to your case with great interest, then cuts the paragraph anyway, plus the one you defended it with.|You fight with your second copy ready. Nessa cuts the paragraph from both.}
opt.left.say.2 [76aa1d] = {“不行。”内莎说，这就是全部论证。|她兴致勃勃地听完你的辩护，然后照删不误，连你用来辩护的那段也删了。|你备着第二份稿子来争。内莎把两份里的那段都删了。}
# en: Let her cut it
opt.right.label [8f19c2] = 让她删
# en: {It reads better without it. You will never forgive her.|The story runs, tighter and less yours. Nessa scribbles "good" in the margin, a word Nessa hands out like gold.|You let her cut it, and slip it into next week's story instead. She notices, and lets it stand.}
opt.right.say.1 [ede85e] = {删了确实更好。你永远不会原谅她。|稿子登了，更紧凑，也更不像你的。内莎在页边写了个“好”，这个字她发得像金子一样省。|你让她删了，转手塞进了下周的稿子。她发现了，也就让它留着了。}

@event lumen_ledger_front_page
# en: Front Page
title [c50df3] = 头版
# en: The press groans like a dragon and spits out the dawn edition. Your story is on the front page, under your own name. Nessa hands you a tack without looking up. "Wall," she says. "That's where front pages go."
text [911ced] = 印刷机像龙一样呻吟着，吐出了黎明版。你的报道登上头版，署着你自己的名字。内莎头也不抬地递给你一枚图钉。“墙上，”她说，“头版都钉那儿。”
# en: A creaking printing press at dawn spitting out newspapers, a student holding the first copy up to the light.
scene [632373] = 黎明时分，吱嘎作响的印刷机吐出报纸，一个学生把第一份举向光亮处。
# en: Pin it to the wall yourself
opt.left.label [ea7224] = 亲手把它钉上墙
# en: You find a gap between a scandal from before you were born and a recipe someone printed by mistake. It looks small up there, and permanent. Nessa pretends not to watch you step back and admire it.
opt.left.say.1 [1fa814] = 你在一桩你出生前的丑闻和一份误印的菜谱之间找到一处空隙。它在墙上显得很小，也很长久。内莎假装没看见你退后一步欣赏它。
# en: Send the first copy home
opt.right.label [a16d32] = 把第一份寄回家
# en: You post it home with a note that says nothing about how long it took. The reply comes within the week: they have framed it, slightly crooked, over the kitchen fire.
opt.right.say.1 [0f1990] = 你把它寄回家，附的字条里只字未提这花了多久。一周之内回信就到了：家里把它装了框，挂在厨房炉火上方，有点歪。

@event verdant_watering_rota
# en: The Watering Rota
title [c1243f] = 浇水值日表
# en: {You're on the watering rota, a chart the size of a door.|The ferns have learned your footsteps and lean in.|You no longer need the chart, luckily, since a fern ate it.} {season == "winter": Frost outside the glass, drips down your collar inside.|{season == "summer": It's so hot the cacti look smug.|{The biting ferns are in a mood.|Something in the far bed grew overnight.|The moonflowers do nothing, beautifully, as they will for years.}}}
text [3987d3] = {你上了浇水值日表，那是一张门板大的图表。|蕨类已经认得你的脚步，纷纷凑过来。|你已经不需要那张表了，幸好，因为它被一株蕨吃了。} {season == "winter": 玻璃外结着霜，玻璃内的水滴顺着你的领口往下流。|{season == "summer": 热得连仙人掌都一脸得意。|{咬人蕨今天心情不好。|远处苗床里有东西一夜之间长高了。|月光花什么也不做，美美地，接下来许多年都会这样。}}}
# en: A student with a watering can and a huge chart in a steaming greenhouse, ferns leaning in hungrily.
scene [535c02] = 热气蒸腾的温室里，一个学生拿着水壶和一张巨大的图表，几株蕨饥渴地凑过来。
# en: Follow the chart exactly
opt.left.label [577433] = 严格按表来
# en: {Warm rainwater for the ferns, cold tea for the moss, nothing at all for the cactus that bites. It takes all afternoon.|By the chart, to the drop. {around(tobias): Tobias says the plants look "right pleased", and they do, in a leafy way.|The plants look pleased, in a leafy way.}|You follow the chart from memory, since the fern ate it. You're right to the drop, and the fern looks unrepentant.}
opt.left.say.1 [02d5da] = {给蕨浇温雨水，给苔藓浇冷茶，那株咬人的仙人掌一滴也不给。整整花了一下午。|按表来，一滴不差。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯说植物们看着“可高兴了”，确实，以叶子的方式。|植物们看着很高兴，以叶子的方式。}|你凭记忆照着那张表浇，因为表被蕨吃了。一滴不差，那株蕨毫无悔意。}
# en: Water by feel
opt.right.label [36cb6b] = 凭感觉浇
# en: {You read the leaves instead of the chart. Everything perks up, and one fern makes a sound suspiciously like purring.|You guess right on every bed. {around(tobias): Tobias asks how you knew. You honestly don't.|You couldn't say how you knew.}|You water by feel and talk to the moonflowers. They still do nothing, beautifully, and the rest of the house thrives.}
opt.right.say.1 [5cac1b] = {你看叶子而不看表。一切都精神起来了，有一株蕨发出的声音很像呼噜。|每一床你都猜对了。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯问你怎么知道的。你真的不知道。|你自己也说不清是怎么知道的。}|你凭感觉浇水，还跟月光花说话。它们依然美丽地什么都不做，温室其余的植物倒是长得很旺。}
# en: {You drown a pot of sundew, and a biting fern takes a polite nip of your thumb.|The moss wanted tea, not water. It sulks visibly, and the cactus you reach past bites.|You water the moonflowers by feel and drown them by feel. They do nothing, as usual, but wetly.}
opt.right.say.2 [4dd1ee] = {你淹了一盆茅膏菜，一株咬人蕨还客客气气地啃了你的拇指一口。|苔藓要的是茶，不是水。它明显在生闷气，你伸手越过的那株仙人掌还咬了你一口。|你凭感觉给月光花浇水，也凭感觉把它们淹了。它们照旧什么都不做，只是湿漉漉的。}

@event verdant_repotting_day
# en: Repotting Day
title [5b9bc1] = 换盆日
# en: {Repotting day. The mandrakes have outgrown their pots and have opinions about it.|Someone always takes the good earmuffs, so you bring your own now.|You know which mandrakes kick, and which only pretend to.} {Tobias's own mandrake watches from a shelf in a knitted hat, superior.|A seed tray of something blue has started to hum.|{season == "spring": Everything is trying to grow at once.|The glass is fogged and the benches crowded.}}
text [56f63b] = {换盆日。曼德拉草长得比盆大了，而且对此很有意见。|好耳罩总被人抢走，所以你现在自己带一副。|你知道哪些曼德拉草真会踢人，哪些只是装装样子。} {托比亚斯自己的那株曼德拉草戴着针织帽在架子上旁观，一脸高傲。|一盘蓝色的幼苗开始嗡嗡作响。|{season == "spring": 一切都在同时拼命生长。|玻璃上起了雾，苗床挤得满满当当。}}
# en: Students in earmuffs wrestling squalling mandrakes into bigger pots on a crowded greenhouse bench.
scene [24e9d7] = 戴耳罩的学生们在拥挤的温室长桌上，把尖叫的曼德拉草塞进更大的花盆。
# en: Take on the mandrakes
opt.left.label [4dbb5a] = 去对付曼德拉草
# en: {One mandrake has rooted into the bench itself. It takes a trowel, a lullaby and both hands to talk it out.|Mandrake after mandrake, each into a bigger pot. Your ears ring all the way to supper.|The biggest one goes in headfirst and comes out, somehow, the right way up. You are not sure who won.}
opt.left.say.1 [20e693] = {有一株曼德拉草把根扎进了长桌里。你用了一把小铲、一首摇篮曲和两只手，才把它劝出来。|一株接一株，全换进了大盆。你的耳朵一直响到晚饭。|最大的那株头朝下进了盆，不知怎么又正着出来了。你说不清是谁赢了。}
# en: Take the quiet seed trays
opt.right.label [62e44f] = 去弄安静的育苗盘
# en: {Hundreds of seedlings, pricked out one by one. Your back will remember this tomorrow.|The seed trays are peaceful and endless. By dusk you have names for most of them.|You do the seed trays with your own earmuffs on, for the peace. One tray hums, and you hum back.}
opt.right.say.1 [5af201] = {几百株幼苗，一株一株地分栽。你的背明天会记住这件事。|育苗盘安静又没有尽头。到黄昏，你给大部分幼苗都起了名字。|你戴着自己的耳罩做育苗盘，图个清静。有一盘在哼歌，你也哼回去。}

@event verdant_tobias_cuttings
# en: The Cuttings Question
title [95ea7f] = 插条之难
# en: {The benches are full and someone must choose which cuttings stay. Tobias can't. "It's like choosing between goats," he says.|Bench day. Tobias hovers over a tray of cuttings, apologising to them in advance and glancing at you.|Tobias hands you the tray before he can change his mind. He is grateful in a way that looks like grief.} {season == "autumn": The frost will take the weakest soon anyway.|}
text [964812] = {苗床满了，得有人决定留下哪些插条。托比亚斯做不到。“这就像在山羊里挑一只。”他说。|整理苗床的日子。托比亚斯守着一盘插条，提前向它们道歉，一边偷偷看你。|托比亚斯赶在自己反悔之前把盘子递给你。他的感激看起来很像悲伤。} {season == "autumn": 反正霜冻很快就会带走最弱的那些。|}
# en: A big gentle student cradling a tray of leggy cuttings, eyeing a full compost heap with dread.
scene [9813c9] = 一个高大温和的学生抱着一盘细长的插条，忧心忡忡地看着堆满的堆肥堆。
# en: Choose, and compost the rest
opt.left.label [0d0865] = 挑好，其余堆肥
# en: {You keep the strongest and compost the rest. Tobias says "fair's fair", then goes very quiet over the heap.|The benches breathe again. Tobias thanks you, then tells you a long story about a goat he couldn't sell. You understand it is about the cuttings.|You choose quickly, so Tobias doesn't have to watch. He takes the compost bucket himself, and you let him.}
opt.left.say.1 [bd94fd] = {你留下最壮的，其余的堆了肥。托比亚斯说“公平就是公平”，然后对着肥堆沉默了很久。|苗床终于松了口气。托比亚斯谢了你，又讲了个关于一只卖不掉的山羊的长故事。你明白，他说的是插条。|你选得很快，好让托比亚斯不用在旁边看。他自己提起了堆肥桶，你由他去。}
# en: Find room for every one
opt.right.label [31bf75] = 给每一株都找地方
# en: {You find room on windowsills, under benches and in a teacup. Nothing grows well, but everything grows. Tobias laughs from the belly.|"Ma always said a crowded field still feeds you," Tobias says, beaming. The benches groan. So does Old Maud, when she sees.|You find room again, on the last windowsill nobody had thought of. Tobias names that one after you.}
opt.right.say.1 [885501] = {窗台上、长桌下、一只茶杯里，你都找到了地方。哪株都长得不太好，但哪株都在长。托比亚斯笑得肚子直颤。|“俺娘说，挤一挤的地也照样养人。”托比亚斯笑得合不拢嘴。苗床在呻吟。老莫德看见了，也在呻吟。|你又找到了地方，在一个谁都没想到的窗台上。托比亚斯用你的名字给那一株起了名。}

@event verdant_greenhouse_keys
# en: The Greenhouse Keys
title [5b68d3] = 温室钥匙
# en: At dusk Tobias holds out the iron key ring, warm from his pocket. "Ma says you give the keys to whoever shuts the gate without being asked," he says. "You always shut the gate." From now on, opening up and locking up are yours.
text [f2975e] = 黄昏时，托比亚斯递过来那串铁钥匙，还带着他口袋里的温度。“俺娘说，钥匙要交给不用人提醒就会关门的人，”他说，“你每次都关门。”从今往后，开门和锁门都归你了。
# en: A heavy ring of old iron keys handed over in a steaming glasshouse at dusk.
scene [0698f9] = 黄昏时热气蒸腾的玻璃温室里，一串沉重的旧铁钥匙被交到另一个人手中。
# en: Open up at dawn
opt.left.label [75086f] = 黎明时开门
# en: Dawn, the glass silver with dew, every plant turning to the light you let in. You had never noticed they all turn at once. For a moment you are the only person in the world.
opt.left.say.1 [186b94] = 黎明，玻璃上挂着银色的露水，每株植物都转向你放进来的光。你从没注意过它们是同时转过去的。那一刻，世上仿佛只有你一个人。
# en: Walk the night round with a lamp
opt.right.label [4c41e6] = 提灯巡夜
# en: You walk the benches with a lamp, checking latches and saying goodnight, because Tobias does and it feels rude not to. Something in the far bed says it back. You decide not to mention it.
opt.right.say.1 [03e898] = 你提着灯沿苗床走一圈，检查门闩，道声晚安，因为托比亚斯就这么做，不说好像不太礼貌。远处苗床里有什么东西也回了一句。你决定不提这事。

@event cartographers_survey_run
# en: A Survey Run
title [4bf18f] = 测绘之行
# en: {Initiates do the pacing. Corvin hands you chalk and a passage to measure before the castle changes its mind.|Your chalk is a stub now, so you mark the turns with notches.|Your stride is a unit of measurement on the master map now, and the castle seems to know it.} {The passage smells of oranges, which nobody can explain.|Behind the wall, a clock ticks that no one has ever found.|Your candle leans, very slightly, toward the left-hand turning.}
text [85a385] = {步测是新人的活。科尔文递给你粉笔和一条要量的通道，得赶在城堡改主意之前量完。|你的粉笔只剩一小截了，于是你改用刻痕标记拐角。|你的步幅如今已是总图上的计量单位，城堡似乎也知道。} {通道里有股橘子味，谁也解释不了。|墙后有一座钟在走，从来没人找到过它。|你的蜡烛火苗微微偏向左边的岔口。}
# en: A student pacing a dark stone passage by candlelight, chalking marks on the wall.
scene [04b918] = 一个学生借着烛光在黑暗的石砌通道里步测，在墙上做粉笔记号。
# en: Pace it properly
opt.left.label [6eb50c] = 老老实实步测
# en: {Every turning chalked and counted. The passage is exactly where it was last month, which on the master map counts as a scandal.|The passage is two strides longer from this end than from the other. Corvin is delighted.|You pace it with the stride the map uses. The left turning, where your candle leans, wasn't there last week. Corvin inks it in.}
opt.left.say.1 [3a9fc5] = {每个拐角都标好数清。通道和上个月一模一样，这在总图上算得上丑闻。|这条通道从这头量比从那头量长两步。科尔文乐坏了。|你用总图采用的那种步幅去量。你的蜡烛偏向的那个左拐口，上周还不存在。科尔文把它画了上去。}
# en: {Your chalk marks lead neatly into a wall that wasn't there when you started.|You lose count somewhere in the dark and have to admit it, which in this club is rarer than a map.|You follow the leaning candle into the left-hand turning and come out a floor below, with no stairs in between.}
opt.left.say.2 [0f3a54] = {你的粉笔记号整整齐齐地通进一堵你开始时还不存在的墙。|你在黑暗里数乱了，只好承认，这在本社比地图还稀罕。|你跟着倾斜的烛火走进左边的岔道，出来时到了下面一层，中间没有楼梯。}
# en: Copy last month's figures
opt.right.label [47f515] = 抄上个月的数
# en: {You copy the old figures and sit in an alcove eating toffee. The castle, as if offended, moves the passage a foot.|The figures match last month's exactly. Corvin looks at them, then at you. "Forger," he says, approvingly.|You copy the figures with your own stride as the unit. The castle, offended, changes your stride.}
opt.right.say.1 [1ae24e] = {你抄了旧数据，坐在壁龛里吃太妃糖。城堡像是被冒犯了，把通道挪了一尺。|数据和上个月分毫不差。科尔文看看数据，又看看你。“伪造犯。”他说，语气里带着赞许。|你用自己的步幅作单位抄了数据。城堡被冒犯了，改了你的步幅。}

@event cartographers_lantern_patrol
# en: The Prefect's Lantern
title [709761] = 级长的提灯
# en: {Every expedition has the moment a lantern comes round the corner. Tonight's has a red sash behind it.|A lantern, a red sash, footsteps. You know this part.|You can tell the prefects on patrol apart by their footsteps now.} {The alcove beside you is just big enough for one.|A loose flagstone ahead clanks if you step on it.|The tapestry here already has feet showing under it, and they aren't yours.}
text [b69280] = {每次探险都有那么一刻，一盏提灯从拐角绕过来。今晚的提灯后面是一条红色绶带。|提灯、红绶带、脚步声。这一段你熟。|你现在光听脚步就分得出是哪位级长在巡夜。} {你身边的壁龛刚好只够藏一个人。|前面有块松动的石板，一踩就响。|这里的挂毯底下已经露出了一双脚，不是你的。}
# en: Students pressed into a stone alcove as a lantern's glow sweeps along a dark corridor.
scene [0eba4e] = 几个学生紧贴在石砌壁龛里，提灯的光扫过黑暗的走廊。
# en: Freeze in the shadows
opt.left.label [df6ae3] = 在阴影里一动不动
# en: {The light slides over you like water off a duck. The footsteps go on.|You don't breathe for a very long time. The prefect sneezes, apologises to nobody and walks on.|You freeze beside the tapestry with the feet. The prefect sees the feet, not yours, and someone else spends Saturday scrubbing pans.}
opt.left.say.1 [47e514] = {灯光从你身上滑过，像水从鸭子背上流走。脚步声继续往前。|你很久很久没敢呼吸。那位级长打了个喷嚏，不知向谁道了歉，走了。|你在那块露着脚的挂毯边僵住了。级长看见的是那双脚，不是你的，于是刷锅的星期六归了别人。}
# en: {The lantern stops on your face. You spend Saturday scrubbing pans and inventing a reason you were inside a wall.|You talk your way out with a story about a lost cat. It costs you the night's mapping and most of your dignity.|The feet under the tapestry turn out to be the prefect's own. You both have some explaining to do.}
opt.left.say.2 [3c0370] = {灯光停在你脸上。你的星期六用来刷锅，顺便编一个你为什么在墙里的理由。|你编了个找丢失的猫的故事脱了身。代价是一晚的测绘和大半的尊严。|挂毯下那双脚原来是级长自己的。你们俩都有得解释了。}
# en: Go back the long way
opt.right.label [457126] = 绕远路回去
# en: {The long way is four flights of freezing stairs. You learn every one of them by heart.|You double back through the kitchens and arrive late, cold and unseen. Corvin raises a mug of milk he certainly didn't pay for.|You know these footsteps. You take the long way at exactly the prefect's pace, one corridor behind.}
opt.right.say.1 [299511] = {远路是四段冰冷的楼梯。你把每一级都记在了心里。|你从厨房绕回去，到得又晚又冷，但没人看见。科尔文举起一杯他肯定没付钱的牛奶。|你认得这脚步声。你走远路，步子和那位级长一模一样，始终落后一条走廊。}

@event cartographers_inking_night
# en: Inking Night
title [e28233] = 上墨之夜
# en: {Mappers ink the master map. Corvin unrolls it, silver lines shifting like breath, and passes you the pen.|Inking night. The map has redrawn a stair since your last visit, out of spite.|The map has started leaving a blank wherever you hesitate.} {"Leave the one behind the kitchens off," he says. "Not for long. For me."|"The stair under the clock. Don't draw it. Don't ask why."|He taps a corridor. "That one stays off the map. Humour me."}
text [7d989b] = {绘图员负责给总图上墨。科尔文展开地图，银色线条如呼吸般起伏，把笔递给你。|上墨之夜。自你上次来过，地图重画了一段楼梯，纯属赌气。|地图开始在你每一处犹豫的地方留下空白。} {“厨房后面那条别画上去，”他说，“不会太久。算帮我。”|“钟底下那段楼梯。别画。别问为什么。”|他点了点一条走廊。“这条不上图。就当迁就我。”}
# en: Two students bent over a huge map drawn in silver ink that shifts as they watch, in a hidden candlelit room.
scene [172414] = 隐秘的烛光小屋里，两个学生俯身在一张用银墨绘成、边看边变的巨大地图上。
# en: Ink it anyway
opt.left.label [f376f9] = 照样画上去
# en: {You draw it, line by honest line. Corvin watches, then laughs, a little too late. "Saint," he says, without the usual smile.|It goes on the map. Corvin is quiet for the rest of the night, which on him is very loud.|You ink it, and the map leaves a blank there anyway, as if it agreed with Corvin. He looks at you and says nothing.}
opt.left.say.1 [b28752] = {你一笔一画老老实实地画了。科尔文看着，然后笑了，笑得晚了一点。“圣人。”他说，没有平常那种笑意。|它上了图。科尔文一整晚都没说话，这在他身上响亮得很。|你画上了，可地图还是在那里留出一块空白，好像它站在科尔文那边。他看着你，什么也没说。}
# en: Leave it off for him
opt.right.label [1f8fe9] = 为他留白
# en: {You leave a careful blank. Corvin squeezes your shoulder and doesn't make a joke, which is how you know it matters.|The map gets a gap and Corvin keeps his secret. You wonder, later, what it's for.|You leave the corridor off. The map closes over the gap so smoothly you'd never know. Neither would anyone else.}
opt.right.say.1 [9ac197] = {你小心地留出一块空白。科尔文捏了捏你的肩膀，没开玩笑，你就知道这事要紧。|地图多了块空白，科尔文守住了他的秘密。后来你想，那到底是为了什么。|你没画那条走廊。地图把空隙合得严丝合缝，谁也看不出来，别人也不会。}

@event cartographers_silver_pen
# en: A Pen of Your Own
title [e2f8fb] = 你自己的笔
# en: Corvin presses a pen into your hand: silver nib, black barrel, warm from his pocket. "Mappers carry their own," he says. "And the first passage you find, you name. Club rule. I made it up, but it's a rule." Tonight you found one.
text [4a3c7e] = 科尔文把一支笔按进你手里：银笔尖，黑笔杆，还带着他口袋里的温度。“绘图员都带自己的笔，”他说，“而且你找到的第一条通道，由你命名。社规。我编的，但它是社规。”今晚你找到了一条。
# en: A silver-nibbed pen laid on a folded map in a candlelit stone alcove.
scene [7722b8] = 烛光摇曳的石砌壁龛里，一支银尖钢笔放在一张折好的地图上。
# en: Name it after yourself
opt.left.label [080f1a] = 以自己的名字命名
# en: Your name goes on the master map in your own silver hand, at the mouth of a passage that smells faintly of rain. The ink shivers, then settles, as if the castle had agreed.
opt.left.say.1 [6ddc6c] = 你用自己的银墨把名字写在总图上，就在一条隐隐有雨味的通道口。墨迹颤了颤，又安定下来，好像城堡同意了。
# en: Name it for a club joke
opt.right.label [cf8658] = 用社里的笑话命名
# en: You name it after the night's worst joke. Corvin laughs so hard a prefect two floors down stops to listen. "Keeper material," he whispers, and for once he doesn't laugh after.
opt.right.say.1 [6bb20d] = 你用当晚最烂的笑话给它命名。科尔文笑得太厉害，两层楼下的级长都停下来听。“守图人的料。”他低声说，这一次他说完没有笑。

@event library_assistant_wandering_shelves
# en: The Wandering Section
title [ed4770] = 出走的书区
# en: {The shelves rearranged themselves overnight. History is missing, and a queue is forming for it.|The shelves have wandered, this time the {poetry|herbals|atlases}. You have brought string to mark the way.|The head librarian simply hands you the catalogue now and points upward.} {Footnote sits where the section used to be.|A first-year swears she saw it go upstairs.|There is a faint trail in the dust, as if something heavy had tiptoed.}
text [c7c525] = {书架一夜之间自己挪了位置。历史区不见了，找它的人排起了队。|书架又出走了，这回是{诗歌区|草药区|地图集区}。你带了根绳子来标路。|图书馆馆长现在直接把目录递给你，再往上一指。} {脚注坐在那个书区原来的位置上。|一个一年级生发誓说她看见它上楼了。|灰尘里有一道淡淡的痕迹，好像有什么重东西踮着脚走过。}
# en: A library aisle with an empty gap where a whole bookcase should be, a student holding a catalogue.
scene [e42286] = 图书馆过道里本该立着一整排书架的地方空空如也，一个学生拿着目录。
# en: Hunt it down by catalogue
opt.left.label [8c8f5d] = 按目录把它找回来
# en: {You find it on the fifth floor, wedged between the cookery books, looking innocent.|You track it by its shelfmarks to a landing that wasn't there yesterday. The queue cheers, quietly, because it's a library.|You follow the tiptoe trail to the atlases, three floors up. They are pretending to be geography.}
opt.left.say.1 [bc8194] = {你在五楼找到它，夹在烹饪书中间，一脸无辜。|你按索书号追到一个昨天还不存在的楼梯平台。排队的人欢呼了，小声地，毕竟这是图书馆。|你顺着那串踮脚的痕迹找到了地图集，在三层楼上。它们正假装自己是地理书。}
# en: {You search until closing. In the morning the section is back where it started, faintly smug.|You find the wrong section, twice. The queue disperses with sighs you will hear in your sleep.|You follow the trail in the dust to a blank wall. The trail continues on the other side, presumably.}
opt.left.say.2 [3c2d26] = {你一直找到闭馆。第二天早上，那个书区回到了原处，隐隐有点得意。|你两次找错了书区。排队的人叹着气散了，那叹气声你在梦里都听得见。|你顺着灰尘里的痕迹走到一堵空墙前。痕迹大概在墙的另一边继续。}
# en: Put up a sign and wait
opt.right.label [f19929] = 贴个告示等它回来
# en: {"Back shortly," says your sign. The section returns in the afternoon. The queue does not.|You sit at the desk and wait. A student asks whether the sign means you or the books.|You put up the sign and the catalogue, open at the right page. The section comes back to see what you wrote about it.}
opt.right.say.1 [d713c9] = {你的告示写着“马上回来”。书区下午回来了，排队的人没有。|你坐在柜台后等。一个学生问，告示说的是你还是那些书。|你挂上告示，再把目录翻到对的那一页摆着。那个书区回来，想看看你写了它什么。}

@event library_assistant_hush_round
# en: The Hush Round
title [bd75f0] = 肃静巡视
# en: {Hush round. One lap of the reading room, a lamp, a finger to your lips.|The reading room has learned the sound of your lamp. Whispers stop a table ahead of you.|The regulars hear your step and straighten up before you reach them.} {A table of second-years is giggling.|An old scholar snores on his notes, in a scholarly key.|Somebody's quill is scratching so loudly it echoes.} {final_year: Soon this will be someone else's lap.|}
text [88e9ee] = {肃静巡视。在阅览室走一圈，一盏灯，一根竖在唇边的手指。|阅览室已经认得你提灯的声音，你还隔着一张桌子，低语就停了。|常客们听到你的脚步，不等你走近就坐直了。} {一桌二年级生笑个不停。|一位老学者趴在笔记上打鼾，鼾声都带着学术腔调。|不知谁的羽毛笔沙沙响得都有回声了。} {final_year: 很快这一圈就是别人的了。|}
# en: A student assistant with a lamp walking between study tables in a dim library, one finger to her lips.
scene [207931] = 昏暗的图书馆里，一个提灯的学生助理在自习桌之间走过，一根手指竖在唇边。
# en: Hush them firmly
opt.left.label [b21b8c] = 严厉地让他们安静
# en: {One look and the room falls silent. Somewhere a bookcase seems to approve.|You hush them. They glare. In the morning one of them leaves a note on the desk: "sorry", in very small writing.|You hush the scratching quill. Its owner hears your step before you arrive and is already writing quietly.}
opt.left.say.1 [77efeb] = {你一个眼神，整间屋子就安静了。某个书架似乎表示赞许。|你让他们安静。他们瞪你。第二天早上，柜台上有张字条写着“对不起”，字很小很小。|你让那支沙沙作响的羽毛笔安静下来。笔的主人在你走到之前就听见了你的脚步，已经轻轻地写了起来。}
# en: Let them be
opt.right.label [29efb1] = 由他们去
# en: {You let it go. The noise rises, the head librarian appears from nowhere, and looks at you, not at them.|You leave them to it. For a while the murmur is the nicest sound in the building.|You let them be. The regulars straighten up anyway when you pass, out of habit, and the noise sorts itself out.}
opt.right.say.1 [42969a] = {你没管。声音越来越大，图书馆馆长不知从哪儿冒了出来，看的是你，不是他们。|你随他们去。有那么一会儿，那阵低语是整栋楼里最好听的声音。|你随他们去。你经过时常客们还是习惯性地坐直了，声音自己就小了下去。}

@event library_assistant_reference_desk
# en: The Reference Desk
title [6eeb1e] = 咨询台
# en: {Clerks take the reference desk alone. The first question arrives before you sit down.|The desk has a worn patch now, exactly where your elbows go.|You keep a list of the strangest questions now, and it is long.} {A professor wants "the green book, about this thick, I read it in my youth".|A first-year wants the book their professor "definitely mentioned".|A visiting scholar wants the one book that explains why bees hum in thirds, by tonight.}
text [5dc304] = {文员要独自值咨询台。你还没坐下，第一个问题就来了。|柜台上如今磨出了一块亮斑，正好是你搁手肘的地方。|你现在专门记录最古怪的问题，单子已经很长了。} {一位教授要找“那本绿皮书，大概这么厚，我年轻时读过”。|一个一年级生要找他们教授“肯定提过”的那本书。|一位来访学者要在今晚之前找到那本解释蜜蜂为什么用三度音嗡嗡叫的书。}
# en: A student behind a reference desk facing a line of readers, one of them holding up a green ribbon.
scene [d1f7a3] = 一个学生坐在咨询台后，面对一队读者，其中一人举着一根绿丝带。
# en: Dig until you find it
opt.left.label [f45a7c] = 一直找到为止
# en: {You find it in the end, in the wrong section, under a cat. The reader looks at you as if you had worked a miracle, and you have.|Three ladders, two catalogues and an educated guess. It is the right book.|There's no such book. You find three that explain it between them, by tonight. The scholar adds your name to the acknowledgements.}
opt.left.say.1 [31df1b] = {你终于找到了，在错误的书区，压在一只猫底下。读者看你的眼神像你创造了奇迹，你确实创造了。|三架梯子、两本目录，外加一个有根据的猜测。就是这本。|根本没有那样一本书。你找来三本，合起来把它讲清楚了，赶在当晚。那位学者把你的名字加进了致谢。}
# en: {Hours later you admit defeat. The book, it turns out, was on the desk all along, propping up the inkpot.|You never find it. You dream of card catalogues all night.|The bees remain unexplained. You add the question to your list, at the top.}
opt.left.say.2 [fcd48c] = {几个小时后你认输了。结果那本书一直在柜台上，垫着墨水瓶。|你始终没找到。你梦了一整夜的卡片目录。|蜜蜂的问题依旧没有答案。你把这个问题加进了清单，放在最上面。}
# en: Send them off with a good guess
opt.right.label [1e3693] = 随口指个方向
# en: {You point confidently at a staircase. They come back an hour later with a different book, which they say they liked better.|"Try the fourth floor." They do. You hope the fourth floor helps.|You send them to the apiary. They come back with a bee and no book, delighted.}
opt.right.say.1 [a45acf] = {你自信地指向一道楼梯。一小时后他们带着另一本书回来，说更喜欢这本。|“去四楼找找。”他们去了。你希望四楼帮得上忙。|你让他们去养蜂场。他们带着一只蜜蜂回来了，没带书，开心得很。}

@event library_assistant_stack_keys
# en: The Stack Keys
title [dd3ec0] = 书库钥匙
# en: At closing the head librarian presses a brass ring of keys into your hand: the upper stacks, the map drawers, the ladder chains. Not the Restricted Section. "Lock up. The shelves settle after dark. Let them."
text [3fbf9b] = 闭馆时，图书馆馆长把一串黄铜钥匙按进你手里：上层书库、地图抽屉、梯子的锁链。不包括禁书区。“锁门。天黑后书架会安顿下来。随它们去。”
# en: An old librarian handing a brass ring of keys to a student among towering shelves at closing time.
scene [5cab1e] = 闭馆时分，高耸的书架间，一位老馆员把一串黄铜钥匙交给一个学生。
# en: Do the closing round alone
opt.left.label [4df85b] = 独自巡视闭馆
# en: You walk every floor with the keys chiming. The shelves shift and creak and settle, like an old house going to sleep, and this time you are the one who gets to hear it.
opt.left.say.1 [185d52] = 你带着叮当作响的钥匙走遍每一层。书架挪动、吱呀、安定下来，像一座老房子入睡，而这一次，听见这一切的是你。
# en: Let Footnote come along
opt.right.label [7a3ccf] = 让脚注一起来
# en: Footnote pads ahead of you up every stair, checking each lock before you do. At the top he sits in the window and lets you scratch his ears, once. You suspect that was the real promotion.
opt.right.say.1 [dd5a65] = 脚注在你前面轻步走上每一级台阶，抢在你之前检查每一把锁。到了顶层，它坐在窗台上，让你挠了一下耳朵，就一下。你怀疑那才是真正的晋升。

@event tavern_cider_rush
# en: The Cider Rush
title [9095b3] = 苹果酒高峰
# en: {The rush at the Gilded Newt. A dozen orders shouted at once, and a tray that holds fewer.|The door won't stay shut. You have started remembering faces by their drink, which works until two people want spiced cider.|The regulars just point now, and you know what the points mean.} {A barge crew has come in soaked and thirsty.|The card table is arguing about the rules again.|{season == "winter": Snow is melting off every coat in the room.|Half the village is on the step.}}
text [926886] = {镀金蝾螈酒馆的高峰。十几份点单同时喊过来，托盘却放不下那么多。|门怎么也关不上。你开始凭各人点的酒记住他们的脸，直到有两个人都要香料苹果酒。|常客们现在只用手指，你也都看得懂。} {一队驳船船员浑身湿透地进来，口渴得很。|牌桌那边又在为规则吵架了。|{season == "winter": 满屋大衣上的雪都在化。|半个小镇的人都站在门口台阶上。}}
# en: A crowded low-beamed tavern, a server balancing a tray of steaming mugs through the press of bodies.
scene [3aaff4] = 低梁的酒馆里人头攒动，一个侍应托着一盘冒热气的杯子穿过人群。
# en: Keep every order in your head
opt.left.label [588aa1] = 全凭脑子记单
# en: {Every mug to the right hand. The whole room tips like it's payday.|You don't miss once. The landlady, who remembers every tab to the penny, gives you a look of professional respect.|The regulars point, and you deliver. The door won't stay shut, and nobody waits more than a minute.}
opt.left.say.1 [1f2c5d] = {每一杯都送到了对的人手里。整屋人给小费都像发了工钱。|你一次也没错。那位把每笔账记到分毫的老板娘，向你投来同行间的敬意。|常客们一指，你就送到。门关不上，可谁都没等过一分钟。}
# en: {The spiced cider goes to the wrong table. The right table has opinions. The wrong table drinks it anyway.|You mix up two orders and three tempers.|Half the village points at once. You misread two points and a wave.}
opt.left.say.2 [246428] = {香料苹果酒送错了桌。对的那桌很有意见。错的那桌照喝不误。|你弄混了两份点单和三个人的脾气。|半个村子的人同时在指。你看错了两个指向和一个挥手。}
# en: Write every order down
opt.right.label [80b465] = 每单都写下来
# en: {Slow but sure. Nobody gets the wrong drink; nobody tips the slow server either.|Your order pad fills with a shorthand only you can read. By closing, you can't either.|You write every order, even the points. By closing you have a full record of the village's habits.}
opt.right.say.1 [100d2c] = {慢，但稳。没人拿错酒，也没人给慢吞吞的侍应小费。|你的点单本上写满了只有你看得懂的速记。到打烊时，你也看不懂了。|你把每一份点单都记了下来，连手指的也记了。到打烊时，你有了一份全村习惯的完整记录。}

@event tavern_regulars_corner
# en: The Regulars' Corner
title [98ab7f] = 常客角落
# en: {The regulars' corner by the fire wants you to sit for a minute. The fire is warm, and the minute is tempting.|The regulars' corner has saved you a stool now, with a cushion on it.|The regulars wave you over by name. They want to know how your exams went, all of them, in order.} {The old barge-man is telling the eel story again.|The herb-wife from the lane wants her pie warmed "exactly as before".|A quiet traveller is listening to everything.}
text [d6ae20] = {炉火边的常客角落想让你坐一会儿。炉火很暖，这一会儿很诱人。|常客角落如今给你留了张凳子，上面还垫了个软垫。|常客们喊着你的名字招手。他们想知道你的考试考得怎样，每一门，按顺序。} {老驳船工又在讲那个鳗鱼的故事。|巷子里那位卖草药的大婶要她的馅饼“和上次一样”热一热。|一个安静的旅人什么都在听。}
# en: An old barge-man, a herb-wife from the lane and a quiet traveller at a fireside table, beckoning a server over.
scene [017fca] = 炉火边的桌旁，一位老驳船工、一位巷子里卖草药的大婶和一个安静的旅人正招呼侍应过去。
# en: Sit with them a while
opt.left.label [c29077] = 陪他们坐一会儿
# en: {You hear the latest instalment of everyone's story. They tip you for listening, which is a better rate than serving.|You sit for one minute and stay for ten. The landlady clears her throat across the room like a falling tree.|You tell them about your exams, all of them, in order. The quiet traveller listens hardest and tips best.}
opt.left.say.1 [9dc187] = {你听完了每个人故事的最新一集。他们为你肯听付了小费，比端盘子还划算。|你说坐一会儿，结果坐了好一阵。老板娘在屋子那头清了清嗓子，像一棵树倒下。|你把你的考试一门一门按顺序讲给他们听。那位安静的旅人听得最认真，小费给得也最多。}
# en: Keep the trays moving
opt.right.label [579530] = 托盘不停手
# en: {You wave and keep going. The corner toasts your back. The eel story will keep.|You work the room like weather. The corner calls you "the brisk one" now, which is not a compliment.|You keep moving. The quiet traveller watches you all evening, and leaves without a word, and a large tip.}
opt.right.say.1 [9813b7] = {你挥挥手继续忙。角落里的人朝你背影举杯。鳗鱼的故事跑不了。|你像一阵风一样忙遍全场。角落里的人现在叫你“利索的那个”，这可不是夸奖。|你一直在忙。那位安静的旅人看了你一整晚，一言不发地走了，留下一大笔小费。}

@event tavern_tab_book
# en: The Tab Book
title [c2811c] = 赊账簿
# en: {Barkeeps keep the tab book, and the tab book says {regular} owes the Newt rather a lot.|The tab book has grown a second volume, and {regular} has a page in it.|{regular} is at the end of the bar, and so, in the book, is a long column of figures.} {{They} smile{s} at you hopefully.|{They} {are} pretending to be fascinated by the fire.|{They} {are} counting coins that clearly won't be enough.}
text [cf577d] = {吧台侍应管着赊账簿，账簿上说{regular}欠了酒馆不少钱。|赊账簿已经续到第二册了，{regular}在里面也有一页。|{regular}坐在吧台尽头，账簿里也排着长长一列数字。} {{They}满怀希望地冲你笑。|{They}假装对炉火很着迷。|{They}在数一把显然不够的硬币。}
# en: A ledger open on a tavern bar, a long column of figures, a hopeful customer at the far end.
scene [1a746d] = 酒馆吧台上摊开一本账簿，一长列数字，吧台尽头坐着一位满怀希望的客人。
# en: Call the tab in
opt.left.label [6ebe68] = 把账收回来
# en: {{regular} pays, slowly, coin by coin, as if each one were a friend leaving. The landlady slides you a few crowns for the collecting.|You call it in. {regular} pays half and an apology. The landlady counts both, and gives you a few crowns of it for your trouble.|You call it in. {regular} pays in coins and a promise, and the landlady accepts the coins. You get a share of those.}
opt.left.say.1 [05b268] = {{regular}一枚一枚慢慢付钱，好像每枚硬币都是一位离去的朋友。老板娘塞给你几枚克朗，算收账的辛苦费。|你开口要账。{regular}付了一半，外加一句道歉。老板娘把两样都点了数，从里头分了你几枚克朗作辛苦费。|你开口要账。{regular}付了些硬币，外加一个承诺，老板娘收了硬币。你分到了一点。}
# en: Let it ride another week
opt.right.label [2f306c] = 再宽限一周
# en: {{regular} looks at you as if you had hung the moon. The landlady looks at you as if you had lost it.|Another week. The column grows, and so, oddly, does {regular}'s loyalty.|You let it ride and add a line to the long column. {regular} buys you a drink with money {they} clearly owe the Newt.}
opt.right.say.1 [f41919] = {{regular}看你的眼神像是你把月亮挂上了天。老板娘看你的眼神像是你把脑子弄丢了。|又一周。那列数字更长了，{regular}的忠诚也奇怪地跟着长了。|你又宽限了一周，在那长长的一列下又添了一行。{regular}用显然欠着酒馆的钱请你喝了一杯。}

@event tavern_behind_the_bar
# en: Behind the Bar
title [7962ab] = 吧台之后
# en: The landlady sets the cellar key on the bar between you. "You pour from tonight," she says. "Barkeeps feed the fire, too. It hasn't gone out in my lifetime, nor my mother's. Don't be the one." The hearth crackles, as if listening.
text [8a0483] = 老板娘把地窖钥匙放在你们之间的吧台上。“从今晚起你来倒酒，”她说，“吧台的人也要添火。这火在我这辈子、我娘那辈子都没灭过。别让它灭在你手里。”炉火噼啪作响，像在听着。
# en: A landlady setting a heavy cellar key on a polished tavern bar, the hearth fire glowing behind her.
scene [818643] = 炉火在后方闪着光，老板娘把一把沉重的地窖钥匙放在擦得锃亮的吧台上。
# en: Bank the fire at closing
opt.left.label [065f76] = 打烊时封好炉火
# en: You stay after the last song, banking the coals the way she showed you, slow and careful as tucking in a child. In the morning it is still burning. You check twice anyway.
opt.left.say.1 [b13718] = 最后一支歌唱完，你留下来，照她教的封住炭火，又慢又小心，像给孩子掖被子。第二天早上火还燃着。你还是检查了两遍。
# en: Pour the staff a round
opt.right.label [82794d] = 给伙计们倒一轮
# en: You pour your first round for the cooks and the pot-boy. The landlady takes hers without a word, then taps the bar twice, which apparently means "well poured" in a language older than the Newt.
opt.right.say.1 [64fdee] = 你给厨子和洗杯小工倒了你的第一轮酒。老板娘一言不发地接过她那杯，在吧台上敲了两下——据说在一门比镀金蝾螈酒馆还老的语言里，这意思是“倒得好”。

@event apothecary_clerk_counting_drops
# en: Counting Drops
title [77984e] = 数滴数
# en: {Old Pell's first rule is to count every drop aloud. Today the counter is lined with tinctures waiting to be measured.|Drop-counting. Your tongue knows the numbers before your eyes do.|Counting drops. Old Pell says you count like a metronome now, which from him is a sonnet.} {A customer is waiting, tapping a coin on the glass.|One of the daughters is humming, which throws off your count.|Today's tincture is the colour of a bruise and smells of violets.}
text [bf51c4] = {老佩尔的第一条规矩是每一滴都要大声数出来。今天柜台上排满了等着量的酊剂。|数滴数。你的舌头比眼睛先知道数字。|数滴数。老佩尔说你现在数得像节拍器，这话从他嘴里说出来就是一首十四行诗。} {一位客人在等，用硬币轻敲玻璃柜台。|老佩尔的一个女儿在哼歌，打乱了你的节奏。|今天的酊剂是淤青的颜色，闻起来却是紫罗兰味。}
# en: A student squinting at a glass dropper over a tiny vial at an apothecary counter, a wall of labelled drawers behind.
scene [44e4e3] = 药铺柜台前，一个学生眯眼盯着小药瓶上的玻璃滴管，身后是一整墙贴着标签的抽屉。
# en: Count aloud, every drop
opt.left.label [2ee43b] = 一滴一滴大声数
# en: {Every vial exact. Anyone in the shop has now heard you count higher than anyone should.|Aloud, drop by drop. Old Pell, passing, nods once without breaking stride.|You count the violet one aloud in perfect time. Pell, passing, nods in time too.}
opt.left.say.1 [9792a6] = {每一瓶都分毫不差。铺子里谁都已经听你数到了一个谁都不该数到的数。|大声地，一滴一滴。老佩尔路过，头也不停地点了一下。|你把那瓶紫罗兰色的大声数完，节拍分毫不差。佩尔路过，点头也合着拍子。}
# en: Count by eye, faster
opt.right.label [f2a5b1] = 目测，快一些
# en: {Quick and true. You clear the counter before the tea goes cold, and a satisfied customer leaves a coin.|By eye, and spot on. One of the daughters raises an eyebrow and says nothing to her father.|You count by eye in your metronome rhythm. Pell checks one vial, finds it perfect, and stops checking.}
opt.right.say.1 [55f6e2] = {又快又准。茶还没凉，你就清空了柜台，一位满意的客人留下一枚硬币。|目测，正中。佩尔的一个女儿挑了挑眉，什么也没跟她父亲说。|你按自己节拍器般的节奏目测。佩尔查了一瓶，分毫不差，就不再查了。}
# en: {One drop over. Old Pell can tell from across the shop. He tips the vial out and makes you count the next one aloud, twice.|You overshoot. The tincture turns from violet to a colour with no name, and Pell sighs like a closing door.|You go by eye and the violet one goes the colour of a worse bruise. Pell makes you count the rest aloud, as punishment and as lesson.}
opt.right.say.2 [975042] = {多了一滴。老佩尔隔着整间铺子都看得出来。他把药瓶倒空，让你把下一瓶大声数两遍。|你倒多了。酊剂从紫罗兰色变成一种没有名字的颜色，佩尔叹了口气，像一扇门关上。|你凭目测，那瓶紫罗兰色的变成了更难看的淤青色。佩尔罚你把剩下的都大声数完，既是惩罚，也是一课。}

@event apothecary_clerk_drawer_check
# en: The Thousand Drawers
title [76310c] = 千屉之墙
# en: {Each shift a clerk checks a row of drawers against the stock book. Today's row starts with one that is stuck.|A different row today, and the same feeling that something in it is awake.|Your row for the week. You have learned that the wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.} {One is labelled in a hand that isn't Pell's.|One is rattling, very gently, as if politely.|One smells of snow, in {season == "winter": a shop this warm|the wrong season entirely}.}
text [c43280] = {每个班次，店员要对着库存簿核对一排抽屉。今天这一排的第一个就卡住了。|今天换了一排，还是那种里面有什么醒着的感觉。|这周轮到你这一排。你已经明白，拉错的抽屉从来不只是拉错了抽屉。} {有一个抽屉的标签不是佩尔的笔迹。|有一个抽屉在轻轻地响，几乎是礼貌地。|有一个抽屉闻起来像雪，{season == "winter": 在这么暖和的铺子里|完全不合时节}。}
# en: A wall of tiny labelled wooden drawers, one of them rattling on its own.
scene [c05965] = 一整墙贴着标签的小木抽屉，其中一个自己在咔哒作响。
# en: Open the odd drawer
opt.left.label [7cc2f8] = 打开那个古怪的抽屉
# en: {Inside, a nest of dried moonpetals that belong on the top row. You move them, and the drawer stops sulking.|You coax it open and find the missing powdered newt. Pell crosses something off a list he has kept for a year.|Inside is a little snow that hasn't melted, and a note in Pell's hand: "Don't." You close it. Pell thanks you, oddly sincerely.}
opt.left.say.1 [599d4d] = {里面是一窝干月光花瓣，本该放在最上排。你挪好了，抽屉不再赌气。|你哄开抽屉，找到了失踪的蝾螈粉。佩尔在一张他留了一年的清单上划掉了一项。|里面是一点没化的雪，还有一张佩尔笔迹的字条：“别。”你关上了。佩尔向你道谢，出奇地真诚。}
# en: {The drawer opens with a sneeze of pepperwort. You sneeze until closing.|Something small and grey bolts out of the drawer and into another, and you never find out which.|The snow-smelling drawer is full of cold. It gets out, and the shop is frosted until noon.}
opt.left.say.2 [f0921c] = {抽屉一开，喷出一股胡椒草粉。你一直打喷嚏到打烊。|一个灰色的小东西从抽屉里窜出来，钻进了另一个抽屉，你始终不知道是哪个。|那个有雪味的抽屉里装满了寒气。寒气跑了出来，铺子到中午都结着霜。}
# en: Sign it off unchecked
opt.right.label [49847c] = 没核对就签字
# en: {You tick the row and move on. Behind you a drawer rattles, as if to say it noticed.|"Checked," you write. Pell reads it, opens one drawer at random, and closes it again with a look.|You sign off the row, cold drawer and all. Pell checks that one himself, as if he'd expected you to.}
opt.right.say.1 [1abc55] = {你打了个勾，继续往下。身后一个抽屉咔哒一响，像是在说它看见了。|你写下“已核对”。佩尔看了看，随手拉开一个抽屉，又关上，看了你一眼。|你把这一排签了，连那个冷冰冰的抽屉也签了。佩尔亲自查了那一个，好像早料到你会这样。}

@event apothecary_clerk_counter_advice
# en: Asking the Dispenser
title [23739a] = 请教配药师
# en: {Dispensers advise, and today a customer asks you instead of Pell.|Pell has moved your stool to the end of the counter, where the customers come.|You know the regular coughs by sound now, before anyone opens the door.} {A farmer has a cough that sounds like a gate.|A nervous student wants something for courage before exams.|A barge-hand has a back that hasn't straightened since autumn.}
text [13f53a] = {配药师要给人出主意，今天有位客人没找佩尔，而是来问你。|佩尔把你的凳子挪到了柜台尽头，客人们来的那一头。|如今常客的咳嗽声你一听就认得，门还没开就认得。} {一个农夫咳得像一扇破门。|一个紧张的学生想要点考前壮胆的东西。|一个驳船工的腰从秋天起就没直过。}
# en: A worried customer leaning over an apothecary counter, a student behind it consulting a heavy recipe book.
scene [7ad077] = 一位忧心的客人倚在药铺柜台上，柜台后的学生正翻阅一本厚重的配方书。
# en: Prescribe it yourself
opt.left.label [34d97a] = 自己开方
# en: {You mix it, label it and explain the dose twice. A week later they come back just to say it worked.|Your remedy, your writing on the bottle. Pell reads the label, reaches for his pen, and leaves it as it is.|You know the barge-hand's back from the sound of the door. The liniment is ready before they reach the counter.}
opt.left.say.1 [2154a0] = {你配好、贴上标签，把用量讲了两遍。一周后他们专程回来，只为说一句管用。|你的方子，瓶上是你的字。佩尔读了标签，伸手去拿笔，又原样留下了。|你听开门声就知道是那个船工的腰又来了。人还没走到柜台，药膏已经备好了。}
# en: {It helps a little, then not at all. They come back, and this time they ask for Pell.|Pell takes the bottle from your hand, adds one thing and gives it back. He doesn't say a word, which is worse.|You recommend the liniment for the barge-hand's back. It's the wrong one, and the barge-hand smells of it for a week.}
opt.left.say.2 [73fce8] = {起初有点用，后来就没用了。他们又来了，这回点名要佩尔。|佩尔从你手里接过瓶子，加了一样东西，又递还给你。他一句话也没说，这更糟。|你给船工的腰推荐了药膏。推荐错了，船工身上那股味儿留了一个星期。}
# en: Fetch one of the daughters
opt.right.label [f428e6] = 去叫佩尔家的女儿
# en: {The elder daughter takes over and talks the customer down like a nervous horse. You watch and write it all down.|The younger daughter mixes it in half the time you would have. She lets you hold the funnel.|Both daughters come, and argue the case across you. You learn twice as much and get the cure from neither.}
opt.right.say.1 [fd0320] = {大女儿接过手，像安抚一匹受惊的马一样把客人安抚下来。你在一旁看，全记了下来。|小女儿配药只用了你一半的时间。她让你帮忙扶漏斗。|两个女儿都来了，隔着你争论起来。你学到了双倍的东西，可方子哪个也没给你。}

@event apothecary_clerk_labelling_pen
# en: The Labelling Pen
title [cbd6f5] = 标签笔
# en: Old Pell is very proud of his labelling. So it means something when he puts his own pen in your hand, its nib worn to his slant, and slides a stack of blank labels across the counter. "New stock," he says. "Your hand from now on. Neat, mind."
text [ea44d4] = 老佩尔对自己写的标签极为自豪。所以当他把自己的笔放进你手里——笔尖已经磨成他的斜度——又把一摞空白标签推过柜台时，这就意味着什么。“新货，”他说，“往后用你的字。写工整点。”
# en: An old apothecary handing over a well-worn pen beside a stack of fresh blank labels.
scene [5f9c82] = 一位老药剂师递出一支用旧了的笔，旁边是一摞崭新的空白标签。
# en: Label the new drawers yourself
opt.left.label [15781b] = 亲手给新抽屉贴标签
# en: You write every label slowly, in your best hand. Pell inspects them through his spectacles one by one and changes not a single letter. His daughters notice before you do.
opt.left.say.1 [a6af78] = 你用最好的字迹，一张一张慢慢写。佩尔隔着眼镜逐张检查，一个字母也没改。他的女儿们比你先察觉到这意味着什么。
# en: Ask him to watch the first one
opt.right.label [bd2847] = 请他看你写第一张
# en: He watches. Your first label wobbles at the end. Pell takes the pen, adds a tiny flourish to your wobble so that it looks deliberate, and returns the pen for good.
opt.right.say.1 [f27abb] = 他看着。你的第一张标签末尾抖了一下。佩尔拿过笔，在那一抖上添了个小小的花饰，让它看起来像是故意的，然后把笔还给你，从此归你。

@event broom_courier_morning_crossing
# en: The Lake Crossing
title [6090d5] = 过湖
# en: {The dispatch bell rings. The village sack is ready, and the quickest way is over the lake.|You have started timing yourself against the gulls.|The lake knows your shadow by now.} {season == "winter": Ice fog sits on the water like a lid.|{season == "summer": The water is so still you fly over your own reflection.|{A headwind is coming off the hills.|Gulls are holding a meeting on your route.|The far shore is lost in mist.}}}
text [03ae44] = {派件铃响了。送往山下小镇的邮袋已经备好，最快的路是飞过湖面。|你开始和海鸥比速度了。|湖早就认得你的影子了。} {season == "winter": 冰雾像锅盖一样压在水面上。|{season == "summer": 湖水静得你像在自己的倒影上飞。|{山那边吹来一阵逆风。|一群海鸥在你的航线上开会。|对岸隐没在雾里。}}}
# en: A courier on a broom skimming low over a mirror-still lake at dawn, a mail sack over one shoulder.
scene [021120] = 黎明时分，一个肩挎邮袋的信使骑着飞帚，贴着镜面般平静的湖水低飞。
# en: Fly straight across
opt.left.label [d53a49] = 直线飞过去
# en: {Straight as a thrown stone. You're early, and the village postmistress tips early couriers.|You skim the water so low your boots leave a wake. Nobody sees, which is a shame.|You fly straight into the mist, by memory. The far shore appears exactly where you expected it.}
opt.left.say.1 [a90b36] = {直得像扔出去的石子。你到早了，小镇的女邮政局长会给早到的信使小费。|你贴着水面飞得那么低，靴子都划出了水痕。没人看见，真可惜。|你凭记忆一头扎进雾里。对岸恰好出现在你预料的地方。}
# en: {A gust drops you into the shallows. The sack stays dry; you do not.|The far shore vanishes and you fly a slow circle round a buoy. You arrive late and very damp.|The mist takes the far shore and your sense of direction with it. You arrive at the right village from the wrong side.}
opt.left.say.2 [e92243] = {一阵风把你吹进了浅水。邮袋没湿，你湿透了。|对岸消失了，你绕着一个浮标慢慢兜圈子。到的时候又晚又湿。|雾把对岸连同你的方向感一起吞了。你从错的一边到了对的村子。}
# en: Take the long way round
opt.right.label [d7b80a] = 沿湖岸绕远路
# en: {Round the shore, safe and slow. The dispatcher checks the clock and sighs.|The long way. You wave to a fisherman, who does not wave back.|The long way, round the mist. The fisherman waves, for the first time. You're so surprised you nearly fall off.}
opt.right.say.1 [e98d91] = {沿着湖岸，稳稳当当，慢慢悠悠。派件员看了看钟，叹了口气。|绕远路。你朝一个渔夫挥手，他没挥回来。|绕远路，避开了雾。那个渔夫第一次朝你挥了手。你吃惊得差点从扫帚上掉下来。}

@event broom_courier_broom_scramble
# en: The Broom Scramble
title [90ac74] = 抢飞帚
# en: {Only one loft broom is in a good state, and every courier on shift wants it.|You have started arriving early for the brooms, and so has everyone else.|You could pick the good broom blindfold, by the smell of its varnish.} {What's left is one that pulls left.|What's left sheds twigs when nervous.|What's left only flies if you're polite to it.} {season == "winter": Frost has got into all their bristles.|}
text [78fa0f] = {阁楼里只有一把飞帚是好的，当班的信使人人都想要它。|你开始提早到场抢飞帚，别人也一样。|你闭着眼凭清漆味都能挑出那把好飞帚。} {剩下的那把总往左偏。|剩下的那把一紧张就掉枝条。|剩下的那把只有你对它客气才肯飞。} {season == "winter": 霜已经钻进了所有飞帚的帚毛里。|}
# en: A draughty loft rack of battered brooms, couriers jostling to grab the least battered one.
scene [bf0220] = 透风的阁楼里一排破旧的飞帚，信使们推搡着去抢最不破的那一把。
# en: Shoulder in for the good broom
opt.left.label [fe1030] = 挤上去抢好飞帚
# en: {You get there first, by a whisker and an elbow. The second-year you elbowed will remember.|You shoulder a second-year aside and the good broom is yours. It flies like a dream; theirs flies like a complaint.|You pick out the good broom by its varnish while the others are still arguing. You're over the lake before they notice.}
opt.left.say.1 [30a9d7] = {你抢先一步，赢在毫厘和一记手肘。被你挤开的那个二年级生会记住的。|你用肩膀把一个二年级生挤到一边，好飞帚归你了。它飞起来像一场梦；他们那把飞起来像一串抱怨。|别人还在争，你已经凭清漆的气味挑出了好飞帚。等他们发现时，你已经飞过了湖。}
# en: Take the difficult one
opt.right.label [c8d2e7] = 骑那把难缠的
# en: {It fights you the whole way across the lake. Your arms will remember this, and so will you.|It takes the whole route to come to an understanding with it. You win, narrowly.|You're polite to the difficult one, very polite. It flies well, and everyone else is too embarrassed to try it.}
opt.right.say.1 [7f0918] = {它一路和你较劲，直到过了湖。你的胳膊会记住，你也会。|整条路线下来，你们才算达成了谅解。你赢了，险胜。|你对那把难伺候的很客气，非常客气。它飞得不错，别人都不好意思去试。}

@event broom_courier_dead_letters
# en: The Dead-Letter Shelf
title [c14849] = 死信架
# en: {Couriers sort the dead letters, post with no proper address, waiting on a dusty shelf.|The dead-letter shelf has grown a second row.|You know which parcels have waited longest now, and which have stopped expecting anyone.} Today, a parcel addressed only to {"the tall one in the red scarf, third tower"|"whoever plays the flute at dawn"|"my brother, the one who never writes"}.
text [56186c] = {信使要整理死信，就是那些地址不全、躺在落灰架子上的邮件。|死信架又多了一层。|你现在知道哪些包裹等得最久，哪些已经不再指望有人来取。} 今天有个包裹，收件人只写着{“围红围巾的高个子，第三塔”|“黎明时吹长笛的人”|“我哥哥，从来不写信的那个”}。
# en: A dusty shelf of undeliverable parcels in a courier loft, one tied with a faded ribbon.
scene [dcc319] = 信使阁楼里一架落满灰尘的无法投递的包裹，其中一个系着褪色的丝带。
# en: Track down the recipient
opt.left.label [6456ac] = 找到收件人
# en: {It takes three towers and a dozen questions, but the parcel reaches the right hands. They open it at once and cry a little, in a good way.|Found them. The look on their face is worth every stair.|You find the brother. He's a porter in the next village and he does write; the letters just never arrive. Now one has.}
opt.left.say.1 [040dc3] = {跑了三座塔、问了十几个问题，包裹终于送到了对的人手里。他们当场拆开，哭了一小会儿，是好的那种。|找到了。他们脸上的表情值得爬每一级楼梯。|你找到了那个哥哥。他是邻村的门房，其实他写信的，只是信总寄不到。现在有一封到了。}
# en: {You knock on every door you can think of. The parcel goes back on the shelf, a little more crumpled, a little more sad.|Wrong door. Wrong tower. Wrong week.|There are a great many brothers who never write. None of them is this one.}
opt.left.say.2 [7d911b] = {你敲遍了能想到的每一扇门。包裹回到了架子上，更皱了一点，也更难过了一点。|敲错门。找错塔。赶错周。|从不写信的哥哥多得是。没有一个是这一个。}
# en: Leave it on the shelf
opt.right.label [52ecc9] = 放回架子上
# en: {It goes back with the others. Some of them have been waiting longer than you have been alive.|You leave it. That night you dream of a parcel on a high tower, waiting.|You leave it, but you dust the shelf. The parcels that have stopped expecting anyone look a little less forgotten.}
opt.right.say.1 [39d2ed] = {它回到了其他包裹中间。有些在那儿等的时间比你活的还久。|你没管它。那晚你梦见一个包裹在高塔上，等着。|你没管它，但掸了掸架子。那些已经不再等人的包裹，看上去不那么被遗忘了。}

@event broom_courier_own_pigeonhole
# en: Your Own Pigeonhole
title [a87c3e] = 你自己的信格
# en: The dispatcher has painted your name over a pigeonhole, in letters that will outlast the loft's roof. "Your route from now on," the dispatcher says. "Village, barge landing, the high towers. When the bell rings for you, you answer." The dispatch bell rings. It's for you.
text [f9338e] = 派件员在一个信格上方漆上了你的名字，那字迹会比阁楼的屋顶活得还久。“从今往后这是你的路线，”派件员说，“山下小镇、驳船码头、高塔区。铃为你响，你就去。”派件铃响了。是找你的。
# en: A courier loft wall of pigeonholes, one freshly painted with a student's name, a brass bell above.
scene [517d9b] = 信使阁楼里一整面墙的信格，其中一格刚漆上一个学生的名字，上方挂着一只黄铜铃。
# en: Fly the route at once
opt.left.label [9240b6] = 立刻飞一趟
# en: You fly your own route for the first time, every turning yours. At the barge landing someone calls you by name to hand over a letter. The wind is cold, and you don't mind at all.
opt.left.say.1 [b37714] = 你第一次飞自己的路线，每个拐弯都属于你。在驳船码头，有人喊着你的名字递来一封信。风很冷，你一点也不在意。
# en: Stand the runners a round
opt.right.label [631a00] = 请跑腿的伙计们喝一轮
# en: The runners toast your pigeonhole with mugs of loft tea. Somebody chalks a little crown above your name. It is still there at the end of the year, smudged, the most permanent thing in the loft.
opt.right.say.1 [0c186d] = 跑腿的伙计们端着阁楼茶，为你的信格干杯。有人用粉笔在你名字上方画了顶小王冠。到年底它还在，糊了，却是阁楼里最长久的东西。
